<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-inverse-laplace-examples" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>More Inverse Laplace Transform Examples</title>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       TABLE REFERENCE
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    Use the Laplace transform table below as a reference throughout this page.
  </p>

  <table xml:id="table-laplace-transforms-ref">
    <title>Laplace Transform Table</title>
    <tabular halign="center" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
      <row header="yes">
        <cell><m>f(t)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>F(s) = \mathcal{L}\{f(t)\}</m></cell>
        <cell>Condition</cell>
        <cell>Formula</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>1</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{s}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>A</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>e^{at}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{1}{s-a}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>B</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>t^n</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>C</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\sin(at)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{a}{s^2 + a^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>D</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>\cos(at)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{s}{s^2 + a^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt 0</m></cell>
        <cell>E</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>t^n e^{at}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{n!}{(s-a)^{n+1}}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>H</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>e^{at}\sin(bt)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{b}{(s-a)^2 + b^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>I</cell>
      </row>
      <row>
        <cell><m>e^{at}\cos(bt)</m></cell>
        <cell><m>\dfrac{s-a}{(s-a)^2 + b^2}</m></cell>
        <cell><m>s \gt a</m></cell>
        <cell>J</cell>
      </row>
    </tabular>
  </table>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       WARMUP: L^{-1}{13/(3s+21)}
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <p>
    As a warmup, consider <m>F(s) = \dfrac{13}{3s+21}</m>. This should match Formula B,
    <m>\mathcal{L}\{e^{at}\} = \dfrac{1}{s-a}</m>,
    but we need a 1 in the numerator and the denominator in the form <m>s - a</m>.
    Factor the 3 out of the denominator:
    <me>\frac{\overbrace{13}^{\text{needs to be }1}}{3s+21}
      = \frac{13}{\underbrace{3(s+7)}_{\text{factor out }3}}
      = \frac{13}{3} \cdot \frac{1}{s-(-7)}.</me>
    Now it matches Formula B with <m>a = -7</m>.
  </p>

  <exercise xml:id="ex-warmup-laplace-13over3s21">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Finish computing <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\dfrac{13}{3s+21}\right\}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <choices randomize="yes">
      <choice correct="yes">
        <statement><p><m>\dfrac{13}{3}e^{-7t}</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Correct! After factoring, <m>\frac{13}{3} \cdot \frac{1}{s+7}</m> matches Formula B
            with <m>a = -7</m>, giving <m>\frac{13}{3}e^{-7t}</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>13e^{-7t}</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Don't forget the <m>\frac{1}{3}</m> from factoring the 3 out of the denominator.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>\dfrac{13}{3}e^{7t}</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            Check the sign of <m>a</m>. Since <m>s + 7 = s - (-7)</m>,
            we have <m>a = -7</m>, giving <m>e^{-7t}</m> not <m>e^{7t}</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
      <choice>
        <statement><p><m>\dfrac{13}{21}e^{-7t}</m></p></statement>
        <feedback>
          <p>
            The constant comes from factoring 3 out of the denominator, giving <m>\frac{13}{3}</m>,
            not <m>\frac{13}{21}</m>.
          </p>
        </feedback>
      </choice>
    </choices>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       EXAMPLE: L^{-1}{(13-3s)/(s^2+9)}
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <example xml:id="ex-inverse-laplace-two-terms">
    <title>Inverse Laplace with Two Terms</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\dfrac{13-3s}{s^2+9}\right\}</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <hint>
      <p>
        <ul>
          <li><p>Split the fraction over the numerator and apply linearity to get two separate inverse transforms.</p></li>
          <li><p>
            Both terms have denominator <m>s^2 + 9 = s^2 + 3^2</m>, so <m>a = 3</m>.
            Check the table to identify which formula each term matches.
          </p></li>
        </ul>
      </p>
    </hint>
    <solution>
      <title>Work</title>
      <p>
        Split over the numerator and apply linearity:
        <md>
          <mrow>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{13-3s}{s^2+9}\right\}
            \amp= 13\,\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{1}{s^2+9}\right\}
               - 3\,\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{s}{s^2+9}\right\}.</mrow>
        </md>
        Both terms have denominator <m>s^2 + 9 = s^2 + 3^2</m>, so both match
        <xref ref="formula-D" text="custom">Formula D</xref> or
        <xref ref="formula-E" text="custom">Formula E</xref>, the difference is what's in the numerator:
        <md>
          <mrow>\frac{\overbrace{1}^{\text{constant} \Rightarrow \text{D}}}{s^2+3^2}
          \qquad \text{vs} \qquad
          \frac{\overbrace{s}^{s \Rightarrow \text{E}}}{s^2+3^2}.</mrow>
        </md>
        The first term needs a 3 on top to match Formula D, so rewrite:
        <md>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{13}{3}\,\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{\overbrace{3}^{a}}{s^2+3^2}\right\}
               - 3\,\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{s}{s^2+3^2}\right\}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp= \frac{13}{3}\sin(3t) - 3\cos(3t).</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       ACTIVITY: L^{-1}{(1+s)/s^3}
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <activity xml:id="act-inverse-laplace-polynomial">
    <title>Find <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\dfrac{1+s}{s^3}\right\}</m></title>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        Work through the following steps.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="task-inverse-poly-split">
      <title>Step 1: Simplify and Split</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Split <m>\dfrac{1+s}{s^3}</m> over the numerator and simplify each term.
          Which of the following is correct?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>\dfrac{1}{s^3} + \dfrac{1}{s^2}</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Splitting gives <m>\frac{1}{s^3} + \frac{s}{s^3}</m>,
              and <m>\frac{s}{s^3} = \frac{1}{s^2}</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>\dfrac{1}{s^3} + \dfrac{1}{s^3}</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The second term is <m>\frac{s}{s^3}</m>, not <m>\frac{1}{s^3}</m>.
              Simplify by canceling one factor of <m>s</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>\dfrac{1}{s^3} + s</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <m>\frac{s}{s^3} = \frac{1}{s^2}</m>, not <m>s</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>\dfrac{1}{s^2} + \dfrac{1}{s^2}</m></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Only the second term simplifies to <m>\frac{1}{s^2}</m>.
              The first term is <m>\frac{1}{s^3}</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-inverse-poly-table">
      <title>Step 2: Identify the Table Rows</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Looking at the <xref ref="table-laplace-transforms-ref">table above</xref>,
          which formula applies to each of the two terms <m>\dfrac{1}{s^3}</m> and <m>\dfrac{1}{s^2}</m>?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices randomize="yes">
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p>Both match Formula C, <m>\mathcal{L}\{t^n\} = \dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}</m>, with different values of <m>n</m>.</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Correct! Both terms are of the form <m>\frac{1}{s^{n+1}}</m>, just with different <m>n</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>\dfrac{1}{s^3}</m> matches Formula A and <m>\dfrac{1}{s^2}</m> matches Formula C.</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Formula A is just <m>\frac{1}{s}</m>, which corresponds to <m>n = 0</m> in Formula C.
              Both of your terms have higher powers of <m>s</m>, so both match Formula C.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p>Both match Formula B, <m>\mathcal{L}\{e^{at}\} = \dfrac{1}{s-a}</m>.</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Formula B has a linear denominator <m>s - a</m>. Here the denominators are powers of <m>s</m>,
              which match Formula C instead.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>\dfrac{1}{s^3}</m> matches Formula C and <m>\dfrac{1}{s^2}</m> matches Formula H.</p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Formula H involves <m>e^{at}</m>, which doesn't appear here.
              Both terms match Formula C with different values of <m>n</m>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="task-inverse-poly-compute">
      <title>Step 3: Compute the Inverse Transform</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Using Formula C, compute <m>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\dfrac{1+s}{s^3}\right\}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <hint>
        <p>
          Match each term to Formula C by identifying <m>n</m>:
          <ul>
            <li><p><m>\dfrac{1}{s^3} = \dfrac{1}{s^{2+1}}</m>, so <m>n = 2</m> and <m>n! = 2</m>.</p></li>
            <li><p><m>\dfrac{1}{s^2} = \dfrac{1}{s^{1+1}}</m>, so <m>n = 1</m> and <m>n! = 1</m>.</p></li>
          </ul>
          Rewrite each term to get <m>n!</m> in the numerator before applying the table.
        </p>
      </hint>
      <solution>
        <title>Full Solution</title>
        <p>
          Starting from the beginning:
          <md>
            <mrow>\frac{1+s}{s^3} \amp= \frac{1}{s^3} + \frac{s}{s^3} = \frac{1}{s^3} + \frac{1}{s^2}.</mrow>
          </md>
          Both terms match Formula C, <m>\mathcal{L}\{t^n\} = \dfrac{n!}{s^{n+1}}</m>,
          with <m>n = 2</m> and <m>n = 1</m> respectively. Rewriting to get <m>n!</m> on top:
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{1}{s^3} + \frac{1}{s^2}\right\}
              \amp= \mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{1}{2!}\cdot\frac{2!}{s^{3}}\right\}
                 + \mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{1}{1!}\cdot\frac{1!}{s^{2}}\right\}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{2}\,\mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{2!}{s^{2+1}}\right\}
                 + \mathcal{L}^{-1}\!\left\{\frac{1!}{s^{1+1}}\right\}</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp= \frac{1}{2}t^2 + t.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

  </activity>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       KEY TAKEAWAYS
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

  <conclusion>
    <title>Key Takeaways</title>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Factor first:</alert> if the denominator has a leading coefficient, factor it out
          before matching the table.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Split over the numerator:</alert> a sum in the numerator can always be split
          into separate fractions, then handle each one independently.
        </p></li>
        <li><p>
          <alert>Identify n carefully for Formula C:</alert> match the power of <m>s</m> in the
          denominator to <m>s^{n+1}</m>, then rewrite to get <m>n!</m> on top.
        </p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </conclusion>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       VIDEO + QUESTION FORM
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->

